            </select>
          </div>

          <div class="calc-field">
            <label for="shiftType">Shift type</label>
            <select id="shiftType">
              <option value="fixed">Fixed night shift (same nights each week)</option>
              <option value="rotating">Rotating shift (nights and days alternate)</option>
            </select>
          </div>

          <button class="calc-btn" onclick="calculateAnchor()">Calculate my anchor window</button>

          <div class="calc-result" id="anchorCalcResult">
            <div class="result-anchor-badge" id="anchorBadge"></div>
            <div id="anchorDetails"></div>
            <div id="anchorNudge"></div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2>3. Fixed Night Shift vs. Rotating Shifts</h2>

        <p>
          These two situations require genuinely different strategies, and conflating them is one reason most shift work sleep articles fall short.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Fixed night shift workers</strong> &#8212; the same shift pattern each week &#8212; can achieve partial circadian adaptation. Over 2&#8211;3 weeks on a consistent schedule, the circadian pacemaker begins to shift later: melatonin onset moves toward morning, core body temperature minimum shifts, and daytime sleep quality improves incrementally. This adaptation accelerates significantly with strategic light management (below). The critical constraint is weekend and day-off behavior: reverting to a conventional sleep schedule on days off &#8212; social jet lag &#8212; disrupts the adaptation and requires the clock to restart the process each working week.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you have the social flexibility to maintain your night-schedule sleep pattern on days off (sleeping 9am&#8211;3pm even on Saturdays), meaningful circadian adaptation is achievable over a month or more on a consistent roster. Without that consistency, you&#8217;re spending every week re-adapting rather than maintaining progress. Our <a href="/blog/why-you-wake-up-tired-even-after-8-hours">explainer on why you wake up tired despite sleeping enough</a> covers how circadian misalignment degrades sleep quality even when total sleep time is adequate.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Rotating shift workers</strong> face a fundamentally different problem. The biological clock cannot meaningfully adapt to a schedule that changes every week or two. Circadian adaptation requires consistent timing signals over at least 2&#8211;3 weeks; by the time adaptation begins, the shift has rotated. For rotating shift workers, the goal is not adaptation &#8212; it&#8217;s minimizing misalignment and managing alertness and recovery within an inherently disrupted system. Anchor sleep is more important here, not less, because it provides the one timing constant in an otherwise variable schedule.
        </p>
        <p>
          One practical implication for rotating shift workers: treat napping as a deliberate strategy, not a sign of exhaustion. A 20&#8211;30 minute nap 1&#8211;2 hours before a night shift begins reliably improves alertness across the shift and limits accumulated sleep debt. The <a href="/nap-calculator">Nap Calculator</a> finds the optimal nap length and timing based on your next shift start and current sleep pressure. For the full breakdown of why that window works and where longer naps start working against you, see <a href="/blog/napping-science">the science of napping</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>4. Light Exposure: The Master Switch</h2>

        <p>
          Light is the primary signal that sets the circadian clock. Getting the timing of light exposure right &#8212; both seeking it and avoiding it &#8212; is the highest-leverage intervention available to shift workers. Nothing else in this list comes close.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/7973321/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Strategic bright light exposure during night shift hours, combined with wearing dark goggles on the morning commute home, significantly accelerates circadian adaptation to night work</a>. The mechanism: bright light (especially blue-rich light above 1,000 lux) suppresses melatonin and sends an activating signal to the suprachiasmatic nucleus. Timed correctly, it shifts the phase of the circadian clock toward the desired direction. Blocking morning light on the commute home prevents the counter-signal that would undo it.
        </p>

        <div class="tip-box">
          <strong>Practical light protocol for fixed night shift workers:</strong>
          <br /><br />
          <strong>Start of night shift (9&#8211;10pm):</strong> Seek bright light. If your workplace is dim, use a bright light therapy lamp at your workstation for 30&#8211;60 minutes at shift start. This sends a &#8220;day begin&#8221; signal at the desired new phase.
          <br /><br />
          <strong>Driving home (6&#8211;8am):</strong> Wear wraparound sunglasses the moment you step outside. This is the single most practical intervention in the entire system. Morning sunlight on the commute is a powerful reset signal &#8212; blocking it protects the adaptation you&#8217;ve built.
          <br /><br />
          <strong>Daytime sleep environment:</strong> Blackout curtains plus an eye mask. Both. Light leaking around curtains is enough to fragment sleep.
          <br /><br />
          <strong>After your sleep window (2&#8211;5pm):</strong> Expose yourself to natural outdoor light. This maintains a light-dark cycle &#8212; just phase-shifted relative to conventional.
        </div>

        <p>
          For rotating shift workers, the adaptation goal doesn&#8217;t apply &#8212; but bright light at the start of a night shift still improves alertness independently of circadian adaptation. Use it as an alertness tool rather than a phase-shifting tool. Our guide to <a href="/guides/light-screens-and-sleep">light, screens, and sleep</a> covers the underlying photobiology in more detail. The <a href="/light-exposure-calculator">Light Exposure Calculator</a> maps your optimal light and dark windows based on your specific shift times.
        </p>

        <h2>5. Caffeine Scheduling for Shift Workers</h2>

        <p>
          Caffeine is well-established as an alertness tool &#8212; but most shift workers use it reactively rather than strategically. The key insight is the half-life: caffeine has a half-life of approximately 5&#8211;6 hours, meaning half of a dose consumed at midnight is still active at 5&#8211;6am. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23204306/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Caffeine consumed six hours before bed measurably reduces total sleep time and sleep quality</a> &#8212; and for a night worker trying to sleep at 9am, that means anything consumed after 3&#8211;4am is working against your sleep window.
        </p>
        <p>
          The framework for shift workers is significantly different from the standard &#8220;no caffeine after 2pm&#8221; advice:
        </p>
        <ul class="shift-list">
          <li><strong>Pre-shift dose (100&#8211;200mg):</strong> 30&#8211;60 minutes before the shift starts. Peak effect at 60&#8211;90 minutes, sustained for 4&#8211;5 hours.</li>
          <li><strong>Mid-shift top-up (optional, 100mg):</strong> At the shift midpoint &#8212; e.g., 1&#8211;2am for a 10pm&#8211;6am shift. This timing allows the caffeine to clear before the sleep window.</li>
          <li><strong>Hard cutoff:</strong> No caffeine in the final 4&#8211;5 hours before your planned sleep window. For a 7am finish with a 9am sleep window, cutoff is approximately 3&#8211;4am.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The <a href="/caffeine-cutoff-calculator">Caffeine Cutoff Calculator</a> takes your sleep window start time and caffeine sensitivity and calculates your personal cutoff. For the full science behind caffeine&#8217;s effect on sleep architecture, see our <a href="/blog/caffeine-half-life-what-drake-et-al-actually-found">deep-dive on the Drake et al. research</a> &#8212; the same paper that quantified the six-hour effect.
        </p>

        <h2>6. Meal Timing on Shift</h2>

        <p>
          Meal timing is the most underrated variable in shift work performance. Eating aligns peripheral clocks in organs including the liver, pancreas, and gut. Misaligning meal timing with the biological night disrupts this synchronisation and has measurable metabolic consequences: <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28164449/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">circadian disruption from shift work impairs metabolic function and predisposes to weight gain and metabolic disease</a>, in part because eating during the biological night raises insulin resistance compared to the same meal eaten during the day.
        </p>
        <p>
          Practically, the advice for alertness and sleep quality is straightforward:
        </p>
        <ul class="shift-list">
          <li><strong>Pre-shift meal (1&#8211;2 hours before shift start):</strong> A moderate, protein-rich meal. This stabilizes blood glucose for the first half of the shift and reduces the need for mid-shift snacking.</li>
          <li><strong>Mid-shift (shift midpoint, optional):</strong> A light meal or snack &#8212; not a full meal. A large meal during the 2&#8211;4am window, when insulin sensitivity is at its lowest and the digestive system least prepared, tends to produce pronounced post-meal sluggishness.</li>
          <li><strong>Post-shift:</strong> Eat lightly if at all. Going to sleep on a full stomach impairs sleep quality for most people. A small, low-glycaemic snack is fine if needed; a full meal is not.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          This is a secondary lever compared to light and anchor sleep, but it compounds the other strategies. If workplace constraints make strict meal timing difficult, prioritising the pre-shift meal and avoiding the heavy 2&#8211;4am meal is the minimum effective version.
        </p>

        <h2 class="bottom-line">The System</h2>

        <p>
          Shift work is a genuinely hard sleep problem &#8212; not because shift workers lack discipline, but because they&#8217;re fighting one of the strongest biological signals in the human body. The evidence points to a layered system: anchor sleep provides timing consistency, strategic light manages circadian phase, and timed caffeine protects the sleep window you&#8217;ve created. Meal timing limits the metabolic cost of working against your biology.
        </p>
        <p>
          No single intervention fixes shift work sleep. The system works when the layers compound: a consistent anchor window plus correct light management plus a caffeine cutoff calibrated to your actual sleep time. The <a href="/shift-work-calculator">Shift Work Planner</a> builds a customised version of this schedule based on your exact shift pattern, outputting your anchor window, light exposure timing, caffeine windows, and meal recommendations in one place. Start there, establish the anchor first, and add the light and caffeine strategies once the core sleep window is stable.
        </p>

